   "text": "The pattern fits a single pseudocubic perovskite phase, and the cell size matches mp-1214655 (Ba2La2Ti2Cu2O11). Peak positions were read by counting along the 0.05\u00b0 grid, so they are good to about \u00b10.05\u00b0.\n\n1. **Observed peaks.** The main reflections sit near 22.4\u00b0, 31.75\u00b0 (100, broad), 39.25\u00b0 and about 45\u201346\u00b0. This is the 100/110/111/200 sequence of a perovskite with a pseudocubic edge of about 3.97 \u00c5.\n\n2. **Ba2La2Ti2Cu2O11 (a = 3.961 \u00c5, c = 16.02 \u00c5).**\n   - Its volume per perovskite unit gives an edge of 3.975 \u00c5, which fits.\n   - The calculated positions agree with the observed ones:\n\n     | Reflection | Calculated 2\u03b8 | Observed 2\u03b8 |\n     |---|---|---|\n     | (100) | 22.43\u00b0 | 22.4\u00b0 |\n     | (110) | 31.93\u00b0 | 31.75\u00b0 |\n     | (104) | 31.74\u00b0 | (overlaps 110) |\n     | (114) | 39.2\u00b0 | 39.25\u00b0 |\n\n   - The overlap of (110) and (104) explains why the main peak is broad.\n\n3. **Sr2TaMnO6.** Its pseudocubic edge is about 4.02 \u00c5. That predicts peaks at about 22.1\u00b0, 31.4\u00b0 and 38.75\u00b0, each clearly too low.\n\n4. **Sr2CaReO6.** Its edge is about 4.14 \u00c5, which puts (110) near 30.5\u00b0. That is far too low.\n\n5. **Unexplained peak.** A weak peak near 38.35\u00b0 (about 12% intensity) is not accounted for by the reflections I checked. It is small and does not justify adding a second phase.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1214655\"], \"confidence\": 0.8, \"note\": \"Peaks at 22.4, 31.75 (broad) and 39.25 deg match a perovskite with a pseudocubic edge near 3.97 A, consistent with Ba2La2Ti2Cu2O11 including its (104)/(110) overlap; the other perovskite candidates have larger cells that shift peaks too low.\"}",
